Authentication methods
    .password-based 
        .most common
        .each user assigned a unique username and password 
    
    .physical identification 
        .uses physical objects like ID, badges, tokens 
        .common in restricted access areas 
    .biometric 
        .relies on unique biological or behavioral characteristics of individuals
        .eliminates the need to remember passwords or carrying physical tokens 
        .requires specialized hardware and accurate data capture 
        .common techniques:
            .facial recognition
            .fingerprint recognition
            .hand geometry
            .retinal pattern recognition
            .signature verification
            .voice recognition



Types of Authentication
.classified based on the number or independent factors used to verify a user's identity 


.Single-factor 
    .relies on one form of verification 
    .ease of access prioritized ahead of security 

.2Factor 
    .requires two different authentication factors to confirm identity
    .typically combines something a user knows and what they have 
    .common in email services, online banking, and enterprise systems

.Multi-factor 
    .requires two or more factors for identity verification
    .designed for environments where data sensitivity and security are critical 

    







Storing Passwords in DBs
    .shows the need for storing passwords correctly in the event of DB getting compromised 


#Hashing Passwords 

    .storing plain text passwords in the DB is a sin 
    .do not encrypt then store 
    .hashing is a one-way cryptographic hash function 
    .hash function provides a many-one mapping between input and output 
        .its impossible to reverse an output 
        . a good function has lesser number of collisions 
            .collisions cannot be avoided due to the pigeonhole principle 
                .talk about the Rainbow Table 
    .salt
        .random data thats concatenated with my password before sending it as the input of the hashing function 
        .not stored in the DB; only present in the application configuration file which is not accessible to outer world 








Sessions vs JWTs
.common methods for verifying a user's identity and control access to applications 
    .session-based authentication - stores user session data on the server and uses a session ID to identify authenticated users 
        .unique session to each user after a successful login 
        .server stores session data, client stores only a session ID(usually )
    .JSON-web authentication - uses a signed token stored on the client, allowing users to authenticate wothout maintaining server-side sessions
        .token-based authentication mechanism used to securely transmit user information between a client and server 
        .contains header, payload, and signature, allowing the server to verify the token without storing session data 
            .after a successful login, the server generates a JWT, which the client sends with future requests for authentication
            .they are stateless, making them suitable for scalable and distributed applications without maintaining server-side sessions 


    .when to use session vs JWT
        .depends on app architecture, scalability requirements, and security levels

        .traditional websites - SBA
        .banking and financial apps - SBA
        .content mgt systems - SBA
        .enterprise intranet apps - SBA
        .REST APIs - JWT
        .single page apps - JWT
        .mobile apps - JWT
        .microservices architecture - JWT
        .cross-domain authentication - JWT
        .cloud-native and distributed apps - JWT 


    

    
